CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a three-dimensional shaded rendering of a curved surface with a flat plane passing through it. The surface is drawn in orange with a graded fill and labeled S near its lower right edge; it arches over like a broad dome whose margins curl downward at the front and the back. A pale blue translucent rectangle cuts through the top of the dome and extends well past it on every side, and the region where plane and surface overlap is tinted a darker olive. A black dot marks the point where the plane meets the crest of the surface, with text above it reading Tangent plane at P0(x0, y0, z0). Two curves are drawn on the surface through that dot, one teal and one dark red, crossing the dome in different directions, and each carries a straight tangent line through the dot that lies flat within the blue rectangle. Text at the lower left reads Curve C passes through P0(x0, y0, z0). Showing tangent lines to two unrelated curves lying in one and the same flat sheet is the whole point of the rendering. The picture defines the tangent plane at a point of a surface as the plane holding the tangent lines to every curve on the surface through that point.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - single smooth orange curved surface S shaped like a broad dome or cap, with front and rear margins curling downward
  - thin darker orange outline tracing the visible boundary of surface S
  - label 'S' placed near the lower-right/front portion of the orange surface
  - black point P0 at the crest area where the selected surface curves meet
  - text label above the black point: 'Tangent plane at P0{x0, y0, z0}'
  - large translucent pale-blue rectangular tangent plane passing through P0 and extending beyond the orange surface on all sides
  - faint white or pale-blue border edges of the tangent-plane rectangle, including a prominent diagonal front edge crossing the dome
  - slightly darker olive/gray-green tinted overlap region where the translucent tangent plane lies over the orange surface
  - teal curve on the orange surface passing through P0, running broadly left-to-right across the dome
  - straight teal tangent line through P0 lying in the tangent plane and aligned with the teal surface curve at P0
  - dark red curve on the orange surface passing through P0, running from the lower-left/front region toward the upper-right/back region
  - straight dark red tangent line through P0 lying in the tangent plane and aligned with the red surface curve at P0
  - lower-left text label: 'Curve C passes through P0{x0, y0, z0}'

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Use a local world coordinate system with P0 at (0, 0, 1.15). Approximate the surface by z = 1.15 + 0.12x - 0.08y - 0.18x^2 - 0.11y^2 + 0.04xy over an elliptical domain about x in [-3.2, 3.2], y in [-2.4, 2.4], clipped to an oval-like patch. The tangent plane at P0 is z = 1.15 + 0.12x - 0.08y. Draw it as a rectangle about 5.8 units by 3.7 units in the tangent plane, rotated so its long visible screen direction runs from upper-left to lower-right as in the source, and so its near/front edge appears as a strong pale diagonal crossing the dome. Surface color is translucent orange with gradient-like lighting, base #f6a14a, rim/outline #ff6f1a, opacity about 0.72. Tangent plane color is very pale cyan #dff3fb with opacity 0.48; where it overlays the orange mesh, the visual blend should read olive-gray #cbd8b6. P0 is a black sphere radius about 0.055. Teal curve: sample on the surface along an oblique path roughly y = 0.10 + 0.22x - 0.045x^2 for x in [-2.5, 2.6], shifted so it passes through P0 at x=0,y=0; it should appear as the shallower, almost left-to-right curve. Its tangent line at P0 lies in the tangent plane in direction approximately (1, 0.22, 0.12 - 0.08*0.22), length about 4.6 units, color #008b8b. Red curve: sample on the surface along x = 0.22t, y = -t for t in [-1.9, 1.55], so it appears steeper from lower-left/front to upper-right/back and passes through P0. Its tangent line at P0 lies in direction approximately (0.22, -1, 0.12*0.22 + 0.08), length about 3.7 units, color #d8243c. Tangent lines should extend slightly beyond the visible colored surface curves, especially to the upper-right side. Place the tangent-plane text above and slightly left of P0 in screen space; place the curve text in the lower-left open area. No coordinate axes are visible in the source.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "planeMode",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "View",
    "options": [
      "Surface only",
      "With tangent plane"
    ],
    "default": "With tangent plane",
    "teaches": "The tangent plane is a local flat sheet attached at P0; fading it in shows how the surface is approximated by one plane near the point"
  },
  {
